<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taiyuan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jinyang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taiyuan is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> province in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fen-river">Fen River</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinyang">Jinyang</a> it was a strategic stronghold for over a millennium: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Li Yuan</a> raised his rebellion there in 617 to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, and it was the power base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> rulers of the Five Dynasties and the capital of the Northern Han until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> took it in 979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taiyuan.jpg" alt="Taiyuan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taiyuan_xiquinhosilva.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
